{
  "_comment": "sc-18810 two-dimensional geometry sweep for mlx:ltx_2_3. Standalone, like the sc-18808 plan:\na video row in config/memory-calibration-plan.json still matches no matrix coordinate (sc-18817\nfolds these in).\n\nDESIGN. Two spatial-latent levels (768x512 and 512x768 are 24x16 = 384 latent cells; 1280x704 and\n704x1280 are 40x22 = 880) crossed with three frame levels each. Crossing two AREAS is what makes the\ncandidates separable at all: with one area the `mpx` column is proportional to the intercept, so BOTH\nthe additive `perFrame` and the `perMpxFrame` cross designs are singular and neither coefficient is\nidentifiable (pinned by scripts/fit-ltx-temporal-form.test.mjs). fps is held at 30 except for one\ndeliberate probe.\n\nRUNGS ARE NOT UNIFORM ACROSS THE ENVELOPE. The LTX VAE decode auto-selects tiling on TWO independent\nbounds, and only ONE of them is machine-independent:\n\n  * the WRITE bound -- `VaeTiling::LTX.writable_frame_cap(h, w) = i32::MAX / (8*h*w)`\n    (gen-core/src/tiling.rs:167, full_res_channels 8) is 682 / 682 / 655 / 297 / 297 over the five\n    declared resolutions. It is a CEILING: no host may exceed it single-pass.\n  * the MEMORY bound -- single-pass `3.3 GB + 340 B/voxel` against `get_memory_limit() * 0.85`.\n    It moves with the host and binds EARLIER on a smaller one.\n\nSo the claim that survives on every host is ONE-SIDED: no host can exceed 297 single-pass output\nframes at 0.90 MP, and smaller hosts tile earlier via the memory bound. Saying instead that tiling\n\"engages at 298 frames on every host\" is false, and this repository's own CI falsifies it -- a hosted\nmacOS runner tiles 768x512 x 97, which is 585 frames below that bucket's 682 cap. `fit` and\n`held_out` points were all rung-1 single-pass decodes ON THE CAPTURE HOST; the `rung2_boundary`\npoints are rung 2 on every host. Fitting one curve across that break would fit through a capability\nchange.\n\nMEMORY IS NON-MONOTONIC IN FRAMES, which is why the frame levels stop where they do. This paragraph\nis PREDICTED from the engine's own committed cost model (mlx-gen-ltx/src/pipeline.rs LTX_VAE_FIXED_\nBYTES / _ACCUM_BYTES_PER_VOXEL / _TILE_BYTES_PER_OUT_VOXEL), NOT measured -- no f297 or f305 record\nwas captured. At 1280x704 the single-pass decode is predicted to cost 3.3 GB + 340 B/voxel x\n267,632,640 = 94.3 GB at f297, the write cap. One lattice step above it, at f305, the decode must\ntile, and the tiled prediction is 3.3 GB + 40 B/voxel x 274,841,600 = 14.3 GB of unavoidable\nfull-output accumulators PLUS 300 B per tile-voxel for whichever tile the budget selects. The\nselector keeps the LARGEST tile that fits, so the tiled cost RISES with host memory: ~15.0 GB where\nonly the smallest selectable tile (192 px x 64 frames) fits, 18.5-21.8 GB across the 384-512 px x\n96-frame range, and ~63.8 GB on the 128 GiB capture host, which affords 768 px x the full 305 frames\nat its 103.4 GiB ceiling. The collapse across the cap is therefore real on every host but far smaller\non a large one (94.3 -> 63.8 GB here), and the most expensive geometry in the declared envelope is\nstill the cap rather than the maximum.\n\nWHAT WAS AND WAS NOT ATTEMPTED is derived from the committed driver log\n(docs/calibration/sc-18810/sweep-run.log), not from this field. `_role` is a PRE-REGISTRATION label\nwritten before any capture; the log is a RUN RECORD. scripts/fit-ltx-temporal-form.mjs reads both and\nTHROWS when they disagree, because they did: an earlier revision of this plan carried\n`not_attempted_host_limit` on 768x512 x 449 and 1280x704 x 241, and the log shows both were begun --\nf449 twice, the second attempt ending in a signal kill after 1238 s; f241 begun at 11:40:16 with\nfree disk at 16 GiB and no terminal line at all, the driver itself not surviving to write one. Both\nnow read `attempted_failed_host_limit`. Note that a `fit` row can ALSO have been attempted and\nkilled -- q8 768x512 x 361 was -- which is exactly why the host outcome cannot be read off the role.\n\nThe host verdict itself stands, and free DISK is what bounds it: on this host (128 GiB unified) a\nsingle-pass decode near the cap drives MLX's elastic allocator cache past physical memory and the box\ninto swap. 704x1280 x 177 (159,498,240 voxels) completed with a decode phase whose peak ACTIVE was\n50.43 GiB and whose END-OF-PHASE cache was 98.24 GiB -- 148.67 GiB as an UPPER BOUND on what\nco-existed, not a simultaneous reading (PhaseMemory::capture pairs a phase-window peak with an\ninstantaneous cache sample). 768x512 x 449 (176,553,984 voxels) was killed by a signal with free disk\nfalling 81 -> 16 GiB. The geometries still marked `not_attempted_host_limit` sit above that line and\nwere not attempted rather than burned. This is a HOST verdict, not a model verdict, and it was\nreached before any curve was fitted -- role assignment here is a feasibility decision and never\nreferenced a residual.\n\nROLES. `fit` trains the candidates. `held_out` is never fitted: a third area (640x640, 400 latent\ncells) and both transposed aspect ratios. `held_out_fps_probe` re-renders an identical\n{w,h,frames} at 24 fps to measure the audio-latent axis GeometryEnvelope cannot express.\n`reproduction_probe` re-renders the geometry behind sc-18809's WITHDRAWN 43.87 GiB figure (bf16,\n768x512 x 145 = the declared 6 s x 24 fps cell) on the production full-A/V path, so the two compare\nonly as an inequality. `rung2_boundary` covers f305 (one lattice step past the cap) and the envelope\nmaximum f449.\n\nDrive with: node scripts/memory-calibration-harness.mjs run --config\ndocs/calibration/sc-18810/ltx-mlx-geometry-sweep.json --backend mlx --provider <name>\n--fresh-per-case --provider-command '[\"<target>/release/memory-mlx-adapter\"]' --sceneworks-repo\n<repo> --inference-repo <inference-at-pin> --resume <out.json> --output <out.json>, with --output\nOUTSIDE the repo and SCENEWORKS_LTX_{REPOSITORY,REVISION,ROOT,TEXT_ENCODER_ROOT} set.",
